$DIR/main.p:11:2: v is passed to v.Free(), which may free what it looks at or move it out (clear(v.data), vec.p:288) here, but a view of what it holds (v.Ptr(0), line 10) was stored in *pq at line 10, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    A call that may free what an owner holds ends every view of that memory
    (language spec section 18.3). The view of v made by v.Ptr(0) at line 10 went
    into memory the compiler does not follow, so it cannot check that the view
    is not used after line 11.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not make that call while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)
